  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSteadier precipitation associated with dynamic lift and warm\nadvection is departing northeast of the region this afternoon,\nwith a swath of partially clearing skies noted from southwest\nVirginia into central West Virginia. This is allowing for some\nsurface-based destabilization as the warm nose that had been\nacting as a lid shifts east. The remnant low-level circulation\nassociated with Isaias is expected to track overhead this\nafternoon, resulting in quite light steering flow across the\nforecast area. Near-surface based convection will focus in areas\nof heating and moisture convergence, with additional elevated\nconvection possible farther north associated with shortwaves\nrotating around the parent circulation. \n\nStorm motions will be very slow given the weak steering flow in\nthe vicinity of the remnant circulation. Rainfall production\nremains efficient, with localized rates that could exceed 2\ninches per hour. A fairly large swath of the area received\nbetween 1.5 and 3 inches of rainfall over the last 24 hours.\nWhile much of this was beneficial, it has left streams and\ncreeks running somewhat higher than normal and soils near\nsaturation several inches down. These antecedent conditions\nsupport a high runoff fraction if heavier rainfall rates \ndevelop, allowing smaller creeks and streams to rise quickly out\nof their banks. Issues should generally remain isolated to \nscattered given the limited footprint of the heaviest convective\ncores. However, should a persistent heavy core track over an \narea that has already seen near 3 inches of rain over the last\n24 hours, more substantial flash flooding would be possible, \nparticularly in the steep Appalachian terrain. \n\nA Flash Flood Watch remains in effect through 9 PM EDT this\nevening for the area of highest concern, primarily portions of\nthe Middle Ohio Valley. Isolated issues cannot be ruled out\noutside the watch boundary as well. Any heavier precipitation\ntapers off this evening, with some upslope showers or drizzle\nlingering in the mountains through Monday morning. \n\nAny tornadic threat has lessened even further over recent \nforecast cycles where it was already low. A brief spin-up cannot\nbe completely ruled out, most likely between Charleston and \nSutton along I-79 if it were to occur, but overall confidence in\nany severe weather is low given limited overlap of surface \nbased instability and the exiting stronger low level wind field.\n\nWinds will turn breezy once again in the higher terrain tonight\nas flow switches around to the northwest behind the departing\nlow. This will likely continue to squeeze out drizzle overnight\ninto Monday morning. Elsewhere across the lowlands some\nadditional patchy drizzle will be possible as cloud bases lower\nto near ground level.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry conditions return Monday through early Wednesday as\ndeamplifying ridging builds overhead. This will yield a very\nwarm Tuesday and Wednesday, with highs in the 80s, perhaps \napproaching 90 degrees in a few spots in the lower elevations. \nThe next opportunity for precipitation arrives with a cold front\nWednesday into Wednesday evening. Despite the warm temperatures\nahead of the front, generally poor mid-level lapse rates should\nlimit destabilization and any significant risk for severe \nstorms. \n\nShowers may linger into Friday as the boundary lifts back north\nas a warm front ahead of a much stronger cold front expected\nnext weekend. This latter system looks to usher in the coldest\nair of the season. Some lake-enhanced snow will be possible for\nthe higher elevations of the Northeast Mountains on Saturday\nalthough with very warm ground temperatures any sort of\naccumulation would probably be a tall order. Could also see a \ngrowing season ending freeze for many locations Sunday morning \nwith dew points potentially dipping into the mid 20s as dry \narctic air arrives.",
